**Rule (Measured context)** · `rule:devperf:measurement` {{ require(quiet.gate_low_s > 0 and quiet.gate_high_s >= quiet.gate_low_s and loaded.gate_high_s >= quiet.gate_high_s, 'quiet and contended measurements must remain distinct') }}Verification gate log artifacts on the shared compute node over the documentation-only corpus measure quiet runs of about {{ quiet.number_forms.gate_low_s | display }} to {{ quiet.number_forms.gate_high_s | display }} seconds, tests about {{ quiet.number_forms.tests_s | display }} seconds and design correspondence about {{ quiet.number_forms.correspondence_s | display }} seconds; contended runs reach {{ loaded.number_forms.gate_high_s | display }} seconds, tests {{ loaded.number_forms.tests_high_s | display }} and correspondence {{ loaded.number_forms.correspondence_high_s | display }} seconds. Measurement sources: {{ quiet.source }}; {{ loaded.source }}. These approximate measured ranges motivate targets, not a controlled profiling baseline or proof of unavoidable cost. Obtain future measurements from (`rule:observe:metering`), retaining the current gate timers until tracing covers them. Record compiler and dependency versions, target, profiles, features, job count, worker CPU/memory limits, cache state, input sizes, service versions, observation mode and source identity in untracked retained run artifacts under .lane/benchmarks. Secrets remain typed under (`rule:observe:privacy`). A clean build has empty target artifacts and compiler-cache misses with dependencies already available offline; a warm one-file rebuild names its changed file and dependency fan-out. Neither includes an undeclared download or silently borrowed build output.

**Rule (Contention and comparison)** · `rule:devperf:comparison` {{ require(comparison.samples > 1 and comparison.relative_percent > 0 and comparison.absolute_ms > 0, 'comparison needs declared samples and practical thresholds') }}Every budget excess is reported even on a cold or contended run. Diagnose contention from recorded concurrent jobs, CPU quota/throttling or pressure, runnable load, I/O pressure, memory pressure and lock/queue waits; a large wall alone is only suspected contention. Record unavailable indicators as unavailable. Keep cold-cache, queued, contended and quiet measurements distinct; do not raise a quiet budget to fit a noisy worker. A comparable baseline uses {{ comparison.number_forms.samples | display }} scheduled quiet samples with identical workload/configuration and declared cache state; a regression finding records a median increase exceeding both {{ comparison.number_forms.relative_percent | display }} percent and {{ comparison.number_forms.absolute_ms | display }} milliseconds. This is a measurement campaign, never an automatic retry of a slow test to obtain a passing result. Preserve every original sample. The import gate is one run; a primary target compilation makes that run cold, and whole-gate excess is a cold-cache measurement on which import does not wait. The invoker records its plan in its append-only declaration ledger before starting, and passes PN_GATE_PLAN as one-run, warm-after-cold-first/second or refresh-first/second. The gate refuses an absent or unknown plan; its first line is GATE-DECLARED, retained with the run log and in .lane/gate-declaration.json before any step. The first member declares the pair before either outcome is known. A warm-after-cold pair has no second member if the first observed cache is warm: there is no different cache state to measure. Its second member requires PN_GATE_FIRST_CACHE=cold from that first receipt. A baseline-identity change instead calls for a refreshing pair, whose second member measures warm reuse even if the first already read warm. Preserve both members where admitted, including the first excess and class; the second never substitutes for the first. A repeat selected because the first was slow or red is a retry. An invocation stopped before any verdict for an external cause recorded in the declaration ledger is an abort, not an outcome; restarting it resumes the same declared run with the aborted log retained. A warm-peer refusal marked unresolved leaves the incumbent intact; only subsequent compatible evidence selected by the declared peer order resolves it, never a retry selected to overturn that refusal. No age-based replacement interval is admitted without a series showing drift. Whole-gate excess uses the tiers' load classes, including load-unresolved when pressure is observed but comparison is unavailable, with cold-cache taking precedence for an observed cold primary build. The quiet-series stall ceiling is established with budget recalibration, not inferred from one gate. Incompatible or missing baselines leave the performance comparison inconclusive while the functional verdict remains unchanged; current budget excess still produces its finding. Repeated comparable observations strengthen attribution, not gate authority. Reviewed baseline replacement states the workload/configuration reason and never erases old findings. The mandatory lint collector and nursery advisory collector keep their distinct compiler arguments and independent target directories beneath the gate target. Neither pass invalidates the other configuration: warnings remain denied by the mandatory pass and nursery remains advisory. Each lint target is bounded by the existing owned-target byte ceiling at its quiescent invocation boundary; no new cache allowance is introduced. A compiler witness alternates both passes, requires each cache to remain fresh, retains nursery diagnostics and refuses a non-nursery warning. Scheduling or cache configuration is part of remediation; do not blame code for an unproved contention cause. Machine compatibility includes the observed CPU model set, allowed CPU count, resource-reading scopes and quiet-admission recipe. Gate comparison baselines use the passive-boundary admission in (`rule:design:development-budgets`); the primary workspace no-run build declares cache state from its current-source, current-invocation successful Cargo receipt. A receipt with compiled targets is cold; a completed receipt with no compiled targets is warm (all requested targets reused); missing, stale or incomplete receipts are unknown. This scope is the primary workspace build, not the isolated fixture builds, which retain their separate compilation accounting. No duration threshold or caller flag supplies cache state. The first compatible retained run is provisional. A warm candidate replaces a cold, unknown or provisional predecessor regardless of their stalls; a cold or unknown candidate never replaces a warm predecessor. Among confirmed warm peers with the same selected cases and comparable step domain, pressure orders replacements only when the quieter run is not slower. If the quieter run is slower and consumes more comparable CPU, retain the faster and cheaper run regardless of its higher stall: it supplies the tighter observed bound on undisturbed equal work, while pressure alone does not observe every interference. If wall and CPU disagree, refuse replacement as unresolved. Name the pressure/cost disagreement on the baseline line. With unavailable or different case domains retain the existing pressure order without claiming equal work; cache-state preference still precedes peer comparison. The same decision selects lineage and prior files. Selection follows retained predecessor trees in the validated import ancestry, preferring warm evidence and then the lowest stall within its state. The baseline line discloses candidate and retained cache states, provisional status (including the retained predecessor on refusal) and the replacement or refusal reason; the compile receipt names the compiled targets. Historical baselines without a cache receipt are unknown, never inferred warm from their walls. During-gate pressure includes the gate’s own waiting tasks, so this ordering is conservative, not attribution to external work. Comparable CPU excludes measured per-commit isolation setup on both sides as well as matched build CPU. Each isolation clone, fetch, checkout and clean reports its waited-child CPU and wall, bound to the invocation and producing step; a locked receipt survives captured output, including both nested tracker-replacement workspaces. Reusing the same commit may avoid transfer work without making program work faster. The removed setup totals remain printed separately; absent historical setup receipts are unavailable, never zero. The tracker replacement case reuses its two owned Git object stores, while its mutation, rendering and equality checks still execute on every run. Comparable CPU always excludes matched build CPU on both sides, even without changed compile inputs; metered isolated compilation is excluded alongside serialized build steps. The mixed benchmarks step reports each Cargo no-run build through ISOLATION-COMPILE, with waited-child CPU and build wall measured before receipt parsing, benchmark execution or validation. Its shared-target configuration is matched on both sides; only the reported build CPU is subtracted, exactly once. Validation remains program work, so a validation regression cannot be hidden by treating the whole benchmarks step as compilation. A rebuilding baseline must not hide subsequent program growth, and a rebuilding candidate must not be charged as program growth. Compile CPU and serialized wall growth remain printed separately for rebuild diagnosis. Boundary quiet is a sampling premise, not task-level attribution or proof of quiet throughout the run. Separately scheduled calibration retains its stricter whole-invocation and suite quiet requirements. A sojka campaign cannot establish a kranich comparison. The tier and whole-gate bounds and pre-move case campaigns remain historical warning declarations until a complete quiet kranich series supports their replacement; gate-17's design-kranich-public-warm campaign is the earlier kranich exception, but lacks the newly required structured scope key. Missing historical node identity is unavailable, not inferred from today's machine. The calibration record states the per-level allowance-retention rule in (`conv:design:case-budget-calibration`). Each step also reports its own CPU against the compatible quiet baseline when available, or against the last retained functionally green run under the same node, lineage and configuration key otherwise, regardless of load. These deltas are labelled unattributed context with no verdict effect: they expose growth without attributing it to code or contention. This separate last-run context is atomically replaced even for loaded runs and never enters quiet-baseline selection. Missing step measurements remain unavailable, not zero. The baseline key names accounting recipe own-cpu-minus-build-and-isolation-setup/producing-step/v1: older totals without measured setup are not reinterpreted as program cost. Only preparation of the gate source is removed as per-commit setup. Git work on synthetic fixture commits remains program work and is printed separately, because constructing those inputs is part of the witness on every run. Cases retain their producing step in comparison evidence; CPU belongs to that step's execution tier, while the receipt retains the case's declared suite tier. If a step has no comparable records, intersect the remaining steps' cases only when no added or removed case belongs to an omitted or unknown step. Subtract exclusive cases' printed program CPU, excluding their measured setup, on both sides; never subtract setup twice or impute a missing case charge. A changed case set does not make aggregate walls comparable.

**Rule (Build configuration)** · `rule:devperf:builds` A build or aggregate gate budget excess opens a misconfiguration finding as well as its measured warning; classify the responsible phase before changing configuration. Investigate dev/test optimisation, codegen units, debug information, link time, incremental reuse, compiler-cache hit rate, enabled features and dependency weight. Keep clean, warm and one-file-edit measurements separate, and vary one proposed choice at a time with comparable workloads. Preserve debugging utility, all required tests, supported features and the exact release semantics. A gate dominated by a necessary full-corpus check calls for that check's profiling or registry review, not an unrelated compiler flag. Missing measurements are an explicit performance-evidence hold, not a timing-derived failure. Findings remain owned until corrected with evidence or covered by an admitted narrow registry entry; contention classification alone does not close them. The six named whole-tree repository checks use their own registered process-and-reaped-children CPU budgets. The command wall remains printed separately; neither figure changes its functional verdict. A measured CPU excess emits whole-tree-cpu-growth and enters the same over-budget ledger. Missing CPU is missing evidence, never zero. The retained step context compares current and previous own CPU with source identity, without attributing a difference to code or load. Per-step budgets fit the retained measurements: the near-quiet hash audit stayed nearly flat as campaign bytes grew, while the remaining checks exercise fixture inventories rather than one uniform byte stream. A byte-scaled allowance would conceal that distinction. Each row records the measured CPU and whether it remains provisional; the provisional rows use retained loaded measurements and make no quiet-calibration claim. The warning margin follows the existing growth margin, rounded upward; quiet remeasurement can replace these provisional values. Case, tier and whole-gate budgets are unchanged.

**Decision (Measured profiles)** · `dec:devperf:profiles` The foundation bite measures Cargo's current dev and test defaults, compares candidate settings on clean and incremental builds plus the same test corpus, and records the chosen settings and tradeoffs as explicit decisions with their data. It then changes the profiles and the build-policy checker together under (`amend:toolchain:development-performance`), retaining defaults only where the measurements justify them. No setting is selected merely because it is reputed fast, and no release setting changes here. This resolves the ownership and closing evidence of record 013's open measurement item; its numerical choices remain held for that bite. No profile or build code changes in this record.

**Rule (Expensive-operation admission)** · `rule:devperf:registry` Unavoidably expensive work is admitted only through the dataset-rendered register below. Each entry mints a stable repository label and states its operation and workload, measured wall and provenance, proposed or admitted budget, irreducible work and attempted reductions, placement, owner and review status. Candidate entries track evidence still owed and grant no exception. Admission requires the owner and reviewer to accept the measured residual cost after profiling; merely exceeding a budget, being contended or calling work important is insufficient. An admitted entry changes only that operation's warning threshold and declared placement, never its verdict or evidence obligations; exceeding its own budget still warns and opens a finding. Unregistered excess is a finding, not a back door to timing-based gate failure. Larger service or benchmark runs may live only on suitable workers, but moving them out of the fast path does not waive their required evidence. Withdraw an entry when comparable measurements fit the ordinary budget, updating citations in the same change under (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`). Do not retain an obsolete expensive exception for convenience.

